Does it give us some clues as to how we can understand it better?

00:36:47.230 --> 00:36:48.960
But that's tomorrow, inshaa'Allah ta'aalaa.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:51.850
Now let's talk a little bit about Arabic before I give you a break.

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:55.350
A little bit about Arabic, an introduction to Arabic.

00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:56.840
It's not an Arabic class.

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:00.540
But those of you that are writing this down, here's what you're gonna write down.

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:03.550
There are three kinds of Arabic.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:07.250
There are three kinds of Arabic.

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:13.340
The first one is spoken Arabic.

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:15.750
Spoken Arabic.

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:22.850
The second one is formal Arabic.

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.340
And the third one is old Arabic.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.890
You can call it classical Arabic, old Arabic,

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:32.250
ancient Arabic,

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:36.350
whatever you wanna call it. So, you tell me now. What was the first one?

00:37:37.930 --> 00:37:39.460
Spoken Arabic. Second one?

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:42.040
Formal Arabic. Third one?

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.460
Old Arabic, classical Arabic. Before we start

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:50.340
looking into the Quran, finding amazement in it,

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:53.840
we have to know that the Quran was revealed in Arabic,

00:37:53.850 --> 00:37:56.350
but we have to also understand which Arabic.

00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:58.950
Because there are at least how many kinds of Arabic?

00:37:59.430 --> 00:37:59.940
Three.

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:03.130
So we have to understand the difference between these three before we get into

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:07.840
the Arabic of the Quran, and analyze and extract benefits from the Quran.

00:38:07.850 --> 00:38:08.840
This is critical.

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:11.030
So let's talk about spoken Arabic.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:15.450
Spoken Arabic is the Arabic that is used when you order a shawarma.

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:21.051
It is used If you're watching an Egyptian soap opera. AstaghfiruLlaah al-'Azeem.

00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:27.340
Uh, if you are, you know, spoken Arabic is what's also called 'Aamiyyah, or Lahjaat.

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:29.930
Right? Uh, this is dialectical.

00:38:29.930 --> 00:38:32.290
So the Algerians have their own, and the Egyptians

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:34.670
have their own, and the Syrians have their own,

00:38:34.670 --> 00:38:38.430
and the Moroccans have their own. The Palestinians have way their own.

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:42.350
This is totally different. Like the Fellahis have their own and whatever.

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:45.040
كَيفَ حَالُكَ. You must have heard that before. كَيفَ حَالُكَ

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:46.640
The Fellahis have "chef haalech".

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:48.950
That's how they say 'how are you?'

00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:52.150
The "ka" becomes "cha". Chef haalech.

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:54.640
The Egyptian says "izzayak beek".

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:57.540
Right? So, uh,

00:38:57.920 --> 00:39:00.950
I don't know where that came from even. I tried finding it.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:02.030
Okay?

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:08.650
So the spoken Arabic is basically street Arabic, you can call it.

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:12.450
Street Arabic, informal Arabic, and everybody's got their own dialect.

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:14.650
Everybody's got their own accent.

00:39:15.120 --> 00:39:17.340
And sometimes they're very different from each other.

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:20.930
Like sometimes an Egyptian, if he's talking to an Algerian,

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:22.140
may have a really hard time.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:26.250
If he's speaking 'Aamiyyah he may have a really hard, like, "What did you say?"

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:29.860
Because especially after colonialism, there's some influences of,

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:32.580
like French and Spanish and other influences that

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:34.840
came into those languages and they got mixed in.

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:39.610
And the Arabs that live in America, they even have, like English,

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:40.740
mixed into their Arabic.

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:44.040
Like I was talking to an imam one time and he's on the phone

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:46.626
and he said to his wife,

00:39:46.626 --> 00:39:48.780
أَنَا لَم أَشُب بَعْد

00:39:48.820 --> 00:39:50.440
I was like, "لَم أَشُب بَعْد"?

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:53.500
What does that mean? I didn't,

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:54.620
what did you say?

00:39:54.643 --> 00:39:56.460
مَاذَا قُلْتَ فِي العَرَبِيَة

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:58.040
He said, "Oh, I didn't shop yet."

00:39:59.720 --> 00:40:00.530
أنَا لَم أَشُب

00:40:00.530 --> 00:40:05.490
[crowd laughs]

00:40:06.190 --> 00:40:09.540
So شَبَّ يَشُبُّ became his verb for shopping.

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:11.120
[laughs]

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:12.810
[laughs again]

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:16.400
Similarly, one of my friends went to Egypt,

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:18.830
and he's trying to rent an apartment.

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.140
He goes "هَذِهِ شَقَّة مُلَيَّت"

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:24.520
مُلَيَّت. And he's like "مُلَيَّت?"

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:26.680
What's "مُلَيَّت"? I've never heard of

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:28.830
Oh مُلَيَّت! "Well-lit".

00:40:29.710 --> 00:40:32.230
[laughs]

00:40:33.010 --> 00:40:35.320
From "light".

00:40:36.010 --> 00:40:37.240
So they

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.530
it's spoken Arabic.

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.320
Okay, there are no rules. You can make it up as you go.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:43.830
Okay?

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:46.740
The second kind of Arabic is what?

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:50.000
By the way, the Quran is not in spoken Arabic.

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:50.740
You should know.

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:52.020
AlhamduliLlaah.

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:54.830
The second is what?

00:40:55.510 --> 00:40:58.510
Formal Arabic. Formal Arabic is the Arabic of the newspaper,

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:00.430
the Arabic maybe of Al Jazeera,

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:02.390
the Arabic of the khaatirah

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:06.280
and the lectures done by most shuyookh. The lectures that you

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:09.250
you listen to, that you download those of you who are students of Arabic,

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:12.540
you listen to lectures of sheikhs in Arabic. That's formal Arabic.

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:16.140
But sometimes they tell you a joke. Then they switch over to 'Aamiyyah immediately.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:18.830
Right. So they do that sometimes.

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:23.360
So, uh, you know, and sometimes I, for example,

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:26.330
I love listening to Sheikh Mohammed Rateb Al-Nabulsi, for example.

00:41:26.340 --> 00:41:27.730
He's really nice.

00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:31.350
Clean. He's like your uncle sitting next to you, holding his arm over your shoulder,

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:32.140
telling you a lecture.

00:41:32.510 --> 00:41:33.600
He's so nice,

00:41:34.510 --> 00:41:34.900
you know?

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:36.830
And you just feel happy and cuddly inside when

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:38.630
you listen to Sheikh Mohammed Rateb Al-Nabulsi.

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:41.930
He's got this wonderful explanation of the entire Quran online.

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:45.410
He actually, we had the honor of him visiting our campus not too long ago in Texas.

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:46.530
He visited, maashaa'Allah.

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:50.110
So anyway, so he's talking and he's giving his lecture and in the middle of it,

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:54.030
sometimes because he gets fired up, he goes back to his Syrian dialect,

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:58.740
right? And in the Syrian dialect they use فِيْ,

00:41:59.290 --> 00:42:04.010
the word فِيْ, which in Arabic, actual formal Arabic it means 'in'.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:08.620
But in informal Arabic, in spoken Arabic, it means 'there is'.

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:10.610
There is.

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:12.820
So he says, في السَّيارة

00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:16.800
في بيت، في شقة.
في this,  في that...

00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:18.150
And I was like, فِي?

00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:21.150
What's inside a car and inside an apartment? He goes, Tthere is an apartment,

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:25.220
you have a this, you have a that. So he uses the word, what? فِي.

00:42:25.900 --> 00:42:27.010
فِي. Okay?

00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:30.610
There's another, there's a crazy story, but I might as well tell you.

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:32.020
The,

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:33.650
the Urdu speakers,

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:38.010
any Urdu speakers here? Like two of you? Very good. Congratulations. Okay, so

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.230
the word  "maafee",

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:43.520
"maafee",

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:45.310
is an urdu word.

00:42:45.800 --> 00:42:46.730
Means "sorry".

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:48.390
Maafee means

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:49.420
sorry.

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:51.820
But "maafee" (مَا فِي)

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:53.220
in Arabic

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:55.400
Does not mean sorry.

00:42:56.300 --> 00:42:57.620
You know what "maafee" means in Arabic?

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:01.430
There's nothing. There's nothing.

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:06.010
Like you go to the Arab guy and you say, uh, Pepsi? "Maafee".

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:08.030
Chocolate? "Maafee".

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:11.420
Okay, Coke? "Fee!" (فِي).

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:14.930
You understand?

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:17.930
So the Urdu speaker says "maafee" means what?

00:43:18.500 --> 00:43:20.830
Sorry. And the Arab speaker says "maafee" means

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:23.520
Nothing. I don't have. There is nothing.

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:26.920
Poor Pakistani guy. He goes to

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:28.000
'Umrah.

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:31.970
And he's standing at the Haram. He's going, "Allah maafee, Allah maafee".

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:33.020
"Allah maafee".

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:38.280
[crowd laughs]

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:39.930
And the shurtee,

00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:44.810
the cop, gets down and he starts beating him. "Fee! Fee!"

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:47.930
[laughs]

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:49.620
So.

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:54.020
But anyway, there's spoken Arabic and there's what?

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:58.190
Formal Arabic. Now I speak some Arabic.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.720
I don't speak spoken Arabic. I do speak

00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:05.230
formal Arabic. Uh, those of you that are going to an Islamic university,

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:07.140
the professor speaks in Arabic. Guess which Arabic?

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:11.230
He speaks formal Arabic. The books and the PhD papers that are written,

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:13.040
they're written in formal Arabic.

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:13.940
Okay, uh,

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:15.860
those of you that are victims of the book called

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:18.880
اَلعَرَبِيَة بَينَ يَدَيكَ, that's supposed to be formal Arabic.

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:24.190
Okay? So formal Arabic is what we find around us in those formal kinds of settings.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:27.420
Modern books and papers that are published are all formal,

00:44:27.790 --> 00:44:28.620
but

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:30.340
I need everybody here to know,

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:33.220
there's a big difference between spoken and formal,

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:37.610
and there's a big difference between formal and what's the last one.

00:44:38.790 --> 00:44:42.300
Old Arabic, ancient Arabic, classical, Arabic. There is also a big difference.

00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:45.250
And I need everybody before this introduction is done to

00:44:45.260 --> 00:44:49.290
understand the difference between formal Arabic and old Arabic,

00:44:49.300 --> 00:44:51.230
because a lot of students make the mistake

00:44:51.230 --> 00:44:52.610
and they don't think there's a difference.

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:57.810
So a lot of students think if they know formal Arabic, that must mean they also know

00:44:58.290 --> 00:44:59.220
old Arabic.

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:04.280
And so what's the common mistake is nowadays formal Arabic,

00:45:04.280 --> 00:45:06.010
they call it الَعَرَبِيَة الفُصخَى

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:08.700
الَعَرَبِيَة الفُصخَى

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:11.200
Fushah is formal Arabic.

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:14.220
And so they say the Quran is in Fushah,

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:16.200
and I'm also learning what? Fushah.

00:45:16.870 --> 00:45:18.220
Which means it's the same.

00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:21.220
Fushah is Fushah. But actually,

00:45:21.790 --> 00:45:25.210
this is الَعَرَبِيَة الفُصخَى المُعَاصِرَة

00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:30.880
And there's الَعَرَبِيَة الفُصخَى القَدِيمَة. There's modern Fushah and old Fushah.

00:45:31.890 --> 00:45:34.680
You have to maintain a difference between those two,

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:37.220
if you're going to make correct analyses of the Quran.

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:40.510
Now, let's understand why there's a difference between those two.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:43.710
The Arabs at the time of the Prophet ﷺ,

00:45:43.740 --> 00:45:46.970
and even before that time, for thousands of years, even before that time,

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:50.210
they lived in a desert. They lived in a place

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:53.170
that did not have a lot of rivers and trees and

00:45:53.290 --> 00:45:56.310
greenery, and they definitely didn't have oil yet.

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:58.410
Like, it was just desert.

00:45:59.890 --> 00:46:00.700
Now,

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:03.200
in that environment,

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:05.270
who were their neighbors?

00:46:05.410 --> 00:46:06.800
The Romans were their neighbors.

00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:11.100
The Abyssinians were their neighbors. The Persians were their neighbors, right?

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:11.740
They had these

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:14.900
kingdoms and empires that were their neighbors.

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:17.900
But everybody left the Arabs alone.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.680
You would think the Romans would want to

00:46:20.680 --> 00:46:23.100
conquer more territory and take over the Arabs.

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:25.810
You would also think that the Persians would like

00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:28.020
to conquer more territory and take over the Arabs.

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:31.060
Because if you know something about big superpowers,

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:33.910
they like to expand the size of their backyard.

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:37.800
So the question is, how come nobody cares about, what?

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:40.620
The Arabs. How come nobody took over?

00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:42.900
And if they wanted to, could they have taken over?

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:47.560
Yes, because they have massive armies, huge armies, and the Arabs had nothing.

00:46:47.560 --> 00:46:48.350
No resources.

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:51.360
There were a Bedouin people, easy to take over.

00:46:51.370 --> 00:46:55.590
The simple answer to why they didn't take over is there was no oil yet.

00:46:55.590 --> 00:46:59.490
[crowd laughs]

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:01.600
Okay, that's a simple answer.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:05.880
Why should we send my soldiers?

00:47:05.890 --> 00:47:09.290
Why should we send our soldiers to get barbecued in the middle of the sand?

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.100
For what? Leave it alone.

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:17.730
And if the Romans tried to take over a little bit, then who will get nervous?

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:19.400
The Persians on the other side.

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:21.930
Because you know, between two countries,

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:25.300
usually there's a border and there's a no man's land.

00:47:25.310 --> 00:47:27.000
I love that name. No man's land,

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:28.500
Right?

00:47:29.080 --> 00:47:31.210
It means, I know I own a lot of land in the world.

00:47:31.580 --> 00:47:32.300
Okay, so,

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:36.300
so the Arab land actually became a kind of no man's land

00:47:37.180 --> 00:47:40.520
because if the Romans move in or the Persians move in

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:44.210
then the other party gets nervous, and it might break out into a war.

00:47:44.210 --> 00:47:45.400
So everybody left it alone.

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:48.900
So the Arabs were basically left alone.

00:47:49.980 --> 00:47:52.430
Now, when you are left alone, simple question.

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:54.210
When you are left alone, who do you talk to?

00:47:55.380 --> 00:47:56.000
Each other.

00:47:57.580 --> 00:48:01.100
You only talk to each other. You don't really talk much to people outside because

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:03.000
you're only mostly spending time with

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:07.090
each other. It's true some Arabs did some trade, but for the most part

00:48:07.570 --> 00:48:08.930
a lot of people didn't come in.

00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:11.190
People only went out and came back in.

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:14.090
So it was an isolated culture. For the most part.

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:20.090
When that culture was isolated, Arabic became more and more and more refined,

00:48:20.870 --> 00:48:23.390
and it took less and less outside influence.

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:30.500
Why is the apartment guy in Egypt saying this apartment is مُلَيَّت?

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:33.190
Why is he saying that?

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:35.590
Because he's influenced by what?

00:48:36.570 --> 00:48:39.030
English. Movies. There's outside influence.

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:41.790
Outside influence affects language, doesn't it?

00:48:42.870 --> 00:48:44.000
It affects language,

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:49.200
but if you have no outside influence, you keep your own original language.

00:48:49.670 --> 00:48:51.400
It's retained, its refined.

00:48:51.970 --> 00:48:56.040
So the Arabs for thousands of years are for the most part left alone,

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:59.480
which means the Arabic gets refined and refined and refined,

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:01.500
and there is no outside influence.

00:49:02.970 --> 00:49:05.280
And this Arabic becomes so advanced.

00:49:06.470 --> 00:49:07.790
You know, when you talk to each other

00:49:08.470 --> 00:49:09.470
for a long time,

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:10.990
you develop your own language.

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:15.590
Like when two people are friends for a long time, they have their own code.

00:49:16.570 --> 00:49:19.360
Nobody else understands that code. They understand each other.

00:49:19.360 --> 00:49:20.290
You understand that point?

00:49:20.670 --> 00:49:24.495
Socially, that's true too. Socially, the Arabs would talk to each other

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:27.110
and they developed this advanced code among each other.

00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:28.940
Only they understand it. Nobody else gets it.

00:49:28.950 --> 00:49:31.080
So when somebody comes from the outside, they said,

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:34.130
that guy is pretty much, he's like, pardon the term,

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:35.790
He's retarded. He's عَجَمِي.

00:49:37.270 --> 00:49:39.700
He's retarded. He can't understand our speech.

00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:42.620
We're way up there. We're speaking a code.

00:49:43.370 --> 00:49:47.000
So they developed this code. It was very advanced. It really was. It's impressive.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:50.540
It's also important to note that these Arabs, what did they produce?

00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:52.280
